  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Monitoring potential for localized urban flooding \ntonight as a warm front lifts north.\n\nA stalled frontal boundary is located over southern Virginia early \nthis morning where slow moving showers are ongoing. Looking at dry \nconditions for most of the area today as the boundary remains nearly \nstationary through this morning. However, the boundary is going to \nstart lifting north as a warm front this afternoon, then make \nsteadier progress through the area tonight. Showers and some\nthunderstorms push into parts of Central Virginia this afternoon.\n\nA cutoff mid-level low over the Deep South today retrogrades north \nthis evening, and that is what will push the warm front into our \narea. As the front presses north, expect additional slow moving \nshower and thunderstorms to develop across central VA, then up \ntoward the Washington DC and Baltimore Metro areas overnight \ntonight into Wednesday morning. \n\nThere is a concerning signal in some of the high res guidance \nthat indicate potential for flash flooding in the metro areas \ntonight. Convection appears to be enhanced from the development \nof a broad wave of surface low pressure, or an MCV that develops\nin the aftermath of diurnal convection. Either way, any slow \nmoving shower or thunderstorm tonight is going to be cable of \nproducing heavy rainfall in a short period of time. Tropical \nmoisture returns as PWATs surge to over 2\" once again. This\nlikely yields high rain rates of 1-3\" per hour in the heaviest\nshowers/thunderstorms. A Flood Watch may be needed for parts of\nthe area, but confidence is too low at this time. Will need to \ncarefully analyze the 12Z and later guidance to determine if the\nthreat for urban flash flooding is there tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances Wednesday \nthrough early next week.\n\nOngoing showers and thunderstorms continue into Wednesday morning as \nthe warm front remains somewhere north of I-66. The cut-off low over \nthe southeastern U.S. weakens/shears while subtropical ridging \nretrogrades toward the Atlantic coast. Increasing warmth and \nhumidity around the west side of the subtropical Atlantic ridge, \ncoupled with weak disturbances aloft will lead to afternoon and \nevening showers and thunderstorms each day Thursday into early next \nweek. Mid-level flow appears strongest Thursday through the weekend. \nGiven the degree of instability with temps in the 90s and 70s \ndewpoints, there could be a risk for severe thunderstorms each day. \nLocally heavy rainfall is going to be possible each day, as well. \nIncreasing warmth and humidity yield heat indices around 100 \nThursday through Sunday.",
